/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  borate01_felmyWeare --- boron in the Pitzer-HMW model, every parameter read
  from its primary: Felmy & Weare, Geochim. Cosmochim. Acta 50 (1986) 2771-2783.

  WHAT THE CASE ANSWERS.  Boric acid is a weak acid that is also a neutral
  molecule, an orthoborate ion, two polyborate ions and (in seawater) two ion
  pairs with calcium and magnesium.  Can the engine's Pitzer model, loaded with
  the paper's tables and nothing else, reproduce the paper's own species
  distribution, and does it then land on Dickson's measured conditional
  constant of boric acid in synthetic seawater?  Two operations, both `speciate` at 25 C:

   1. borax_table1 -- water saturated with borax (Na2B4O7.10H2O).  Totals: Na
      0.323 and boron 0.646 mol/kg (Na : B = 1 : 2, the borax stoichiometry,
      `totalsBasis stoichiometric`); the pH is SOLVED.  Network: water
      dissociation, B(OH)4-, B3O3(OH)4-, B4O5(OH)4 2-.
      Table 1 (p. 2772) prints the answer:  B(OH)3 .081, B(OH)4- .131,
      B3O3(OH)4- .050, B4O5(OH)4 2- .071 mol/kg.
      Choupo:  .0794, .1311, .0517, .0701 (pH 9.27).
   2. seawater_boron_pH82 -- Dickson's synthetic seawater, S = 35 (Dickson 1990,
      Table 1, p. 756: NaCl, Na2SO4, KCl, MgCl2, CaCl2; ionic strength 0.7225,
      the engine gets 0.7225), at the GIVEN pH 8.2, with the posture of the
      parameterisation: the major ions unpaired, CaB(OH)4+ and MgB(OH)4+
      explicit, and bisulfate added because Dickson's hydrogen ion scale counts
      the proton bound to sulfate.  Dickson's Table 3 (p. 762) prints
      ln(K* / m0) = -19.7556 (total scale, molality, +-0.004); the engine gives
      -19.7115, 0.044 higher: m_H3BO3 0.85 % below what the paper's constant
      implies.

  WHAT IS HELD, AND HOW FAR IT CAN BE TRUSTED (read this before the numbers).
   * The ANCHOR rows of the `expected` file are the five numbers of the paper's
     Table 1, which is CALCULATED BY THE PAPER'S OWN MODEL, from the very
     parameters that were fitted to the data: it is IN-SAMPLE.  Its agreement
     shows that the tables were transcribed and the engine implements the same
     sums (neutral lambda and zeta terms, ion pairs, polyborate virials); it
     does not test the model against nature.
   * The seawater row is an anchor with the comparison built on the value READ
     from Dickson (1990), Table 3, p. 762.  The conversion to what the engine
     computes is written out in the `expected` file: the engine's pH is the
     free hydrogen ion, the paper's the total (free plus bisulfate), so the
     engine's own bisulfate is added; the tolerance (1.2 %) is twice the
     root-sum-square of the paper's uncertainty, its fit residual, the
     bisulfate conversion and the borate paper's emf scatter.  The model is
     outside the paper's stated uncertainty (0.044 in ln K* against 0.004) and
     inside the tolerance.  It is PARTLY independent: Dickson's seawater
     measurements are not among the data the borate paper fitted (its
     Platford, Hershey et al. 1985 and unpublished Dickson KCl data), but the
     Na, Ca and Mg borate terms rest on data of the same family.
   * The formation constants of B(OH)4-, the polyborates and the two ion pairs
     are DERIVED from the standard chemical potentials of Felmy & Weare
     Table 2 (p. 2773) and Harvie, Moller & Weare (1984) Table 4 (p. 728), read
     from page images; each agrees with the public-domain databank's constant
     to 4e-4 in log K (data/curation/batches/boron_c198/formation_check.tsv).
   * 25 C only.  The paper gives no temperature dependence; the engine announces
     any run away from 298.15 K.
   * The case is not sealed: the records come from data/standards.  Not read
     here: the Searles Lake application (Tables 6-14, solids), which needs
     mineral records the engine does not carry for these salts.

  The pH scale is the HMW one (free H+ activity, single-ion convention); the
  seawater pH 8.2 is GIVEN on that scale.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    // -- 1. Borax-saturated water, 25 C: the species distribution of Table 1 (p. 2772).
    //    Totals are the stoichiometry of Na2B4O7: Na = 0.323 and
    //    boron = 0.081 + 0.131 + 3 x 0.050 + 4 x 0.071 = 0.646 mol/kg (Na : B = 1 : 2).
    //    The pH is not given: electroneutrality closes it.
    {
        name        borax_table1;
        type        speciate;
        temperature 298.15;
        analyticalTotals      { Na 0.323 mol/kg; H3BO3 0.646 mol/kg; }
        totalsBasis stoichiometric;   // what was dissolved (borax), not a measured analysis
        pH          solve;
        networkScope restricted;
        network      ( water-dissociation BOH4-formation B3O3OH4-formation B4O5OH4-formation );
        reason       "the borate network of the Felmy & Weare parameterisation: boric acid, the orthoborate and the two polyborates; no ion pairing of the major ions";
        diagSpecies ( Na H3BO3 BOH4 B3O3OH4 B4O5OH4 H );
        output      { file borax_table1.csv; }
    }

    // -- 2. Dickson's synthetic seawater, S = 35, 25 C (Dickson 1990, Table 1, p. 756, which has no
    //    bromide, strontium, fluoride or carbonate): NaCl 0.42764, Na2SO4 0.02927, KCl 0.01058,
    //    MgCl2 0.05474, CaCl2 0.01075 mol/kg, i.e. the totals below.  Boron at the seawater level;
    //    the pH is GIVEN, the constant does not depend on it.  Bisulfate is in the network: the
    //    "total" hydrogen ion of Dickson's scale is the free H+ plus the H+ bound to sulfate.
    {
        name        seawater_boron_pH82;
        type        speciate;
        temperature 298.15;
        analyticalTotals { Na 0.48618 mol/kg; Mg 0.05474 mol/kg; Ca 0.01075 mol/kg;
                           K 0.01058 mol/kg; Cl 0.5692 mol/kg; SO4 0.02927 mol/kg;
                           H3BO3 4.3116e-4 mol/kg; }
        pH          8.2;
        networkScope restricted;
        network      ( water-dissociation BOH4-formation CaBOH4-formation MgBOH4-formation HSO4-formation );
        reason       "the posture of the parameterisation (the major ions unpaired, the calcium and magnesium borate ion pairs explicit) plus bisulfate, because Dickson's hydrogen ion scale counts the proton bound to sulfate";
        diagSpecies ( H H3BO3 BOH4 CaBOH4 MgBOH4 HSO4 );
        output      { file seawater_boron_pH82.csv; }
    }
);
